1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,876
In the year 756 of our common era, an impossible crisis gripped the royal court of the Rashtrakuta Empire.

2
00:00:06,876 --> 00:00:14,114
Deep within the stone corridors of the capital, King Krishna the First lay struck down by a mysterious, wasting illness.

3
00:00:14,114 --> 00:00:15,924
Imperial physicians could do nothing.

4
00:00:15,924 --> 00:00:20,990
With each passing night, the ruler of western India slipped closer to the grave.

5
00:00:20,990 --> 00:00:26,419
According to the ancient chronicles of the Katha-Kalpataru, his queen made a desperate, terrifying vow.

6
00:00:26,419 --> 00:00:37,638
She swore an oath to Shiva: she would touch no food, she would fast to death, until she could gaze upon the finished spire of a temple raised in his honor.

7
00:00:37,638 --> 00:00:39,085
The King miraculously recovered.

8
00:00:39,085 --> 00:00:40,534
The royal court rejoiced.

9
00:00:40,534 --> 00:00:43,066
But their celebration turned instantly to horror.

10
00:00:43,066 --> 00:00:44,876
The queen was already weakening.

11
00:00:44,876 --> 00:00:47,047
The vow could not be broken.

12
00:00:47,047 --> 00:00:52,114
She would not eat until she saw the shikhara, the pinnacle of the temple.

13
00:00:52,114 --> 00:00:55,734
The King summoned the greatest master builders of the Deccan.

14
00:00:55,734 --> 00:00:57,543
He demanded an immediate solution.

15
00:00:57,543 --> 00:00:59,715
Their reply was a death sentence.

16
00:00:59,715 --> 00:01:05,867
To quarry stone, build foundations, and erect timber scaffolding to raise a spire would take twenty years.

17
00:01:05,867 --> 00:01:11,657
Before the foundation stones could even settle into the earth, the queen would starve to death.

18
00:01:11,657 --> 00:01:19,862
Into the despair of the imperial council stepped a solitary master builder whose name would live for twelve hundred years: Kokasa.

19
00:01:19,862 --> 00:01:28,459
Kokasa was a master Sthapati from central India, an engineer who did not merely follow tradition; he commanded the laws of physics.

20
00:01:28,459 --> 00:01:37,837
Before the astonished king, Kokasa made an outrageous declaration: 'Your Majesty, the Queen will behold the temple's pinnacle in seven days.' The court scoffed.

21
00:01:37,837 --> 00:01:38,618
Seven days?

22
00:01:38,618 --> 00:01:44,090
Seven days was impossible even to clear the brushwood, let alone build a spire.

23
00:01:44,090 --> 00:01:51,514
Every temple in the world was additive: quarried blocks stacked from the ground up, requiring years of wooden scaffolding.

24
00:01:51,514 --> 00:01:53,858
Kokasa's plan was pure subtractive heresy.

25
00:01:53,858 --> 00:01:55,812
They would not build up.

26
00:01:55,812 --> 00:01:59,720
They would climb to the mountain's crest, and carve downward.

27
00:01:59,720 --> 00:02:05,190
By starting at the very summit, the spire, the shikhara, would be carved first.

28
00:02:05,190 --> 00:02:12,224
In one week, the queen could look up from the valley and see it gleaming in the sun.

29
00:02:12,224 --> 00:02:16,522
This was the secret doctrine of the Shilpa Shastras: deductive excavation.

30
00:02:16,522 --> 00:02:22,384
The mountain was not raw material; it was the temple itself, waiting to be freed.

31
00:02:22,384 --> 00:02:23,165
No scaffolding.

32
00:02:23,165 --> 00:02:25,510
No hauling granite from distant quarries.

33
00:02:25,510 --> 00:02:29,418
The mountain's own stone would serve as its working platform.

34
00:02:29,418 --> 00:02:32,543
The King made his choice: 'Find the mountain.

35
00:02:32,543 --> 00:02:33,716
We begin immediately.'

36
00:02:33,716 --> 00:02:38,051
Kokasa rode out into the rugged expanse of the Western Deccan.

37
00:02:38,051 --> 00:02:43,175
He was not looking for beauty; he was looking for raw lithological strength.

38
00:02:43,175 --> 00:02:49,481
Sixty-six million years earlier, at the end of the age of dinosaurs, the earth tore open.

39
00:02:49,481 --> 00:02:53,423
Millions of cubic kilometers of molten lava flooded the region.

40
00:02:53,423 --> 00:03:00,911
Layer upon layer cooled into stacked horizontal sheets of tholeiitic basalt, creating the towering terraces of the Sahyadri mountains.

41
00:03:00,911 --> 00:03:04,064
Kokasa arrived at Elapura, the sacred Charanandri ridge.

42
00:03:04,064 --> 00:03:08,006
A gently sloping escarpment rising thirty meters above the plain.

43
00:03:08,006 --> 00:03:16,677
The location was geographically perfect: it stood adjacent to the sacred Shiva shrine of Ghrishneshwar, and directly guarded the Dakshinapatha trade corridor.

44
00:03:16,677 --> 00:03:21,406
Existing caves had already been carved here by Buddhist and Hindu ascetics.

45
00:03:21,406 --> 00:03:23,771
Kokasa knew the rock held together.

46
00:03:23,771 --> 00:03:26,924
But was it strong enough for a monolith?

47
00:03:26,924 --> 00:03:31,654
He took a bronze testing mallet and struck the upper rock shelf.

48
00:03:31,654 --> 00:03:40,325
If the stone rang with a dull, hollow thud, it was amygdaloidal, full of weak zeolite bubbles that would shatter under stress.

49
00:03:40,325 --> 00:03:43,872
He moved further down the ridge and struck again.

50
00:03:43,872 --> 00:03:49,784
This time, the stone rang out like a temple bell: a clear, high-pitched metallic chime.

51
00:03:49,784 --> 00:03:51,755
This was dense microcrystalline basalt.

52
00:03:51,755 --> 00:03:58,061
Its compressive strength exceeded one hundred and forty megapascals, twice as strong as modern structural concrete.

53
00:03:58,061 --> 00:04:01,609
Kokasa turned to his apprentices: 'Here is our mountain.

54
00:04:01,609 --> 00:04:04,367
It will hold the house of Shiva.'

55
00:04:04,367 --> 00:04:12,650
Carving four hundred thousand tons of mountain is not merely an act of faith; it is an astronomical economic undertaking.

56
00:04:12,650 --> 00:04:15,964
In modern terms, Kailasa was a billion-dollar megaproject.

57
00:04:15,964 --> 00:04:19,692
Where did King Krishna the First find such capital?

58
00:04:19,692 --> 00:04:20,934
First: Imperial conquest.

59
00:04:20,934 --> 00:04:28,804
Krishna had just crushed the legendary Chalukyas of Badami, looting their royal treasuries and seizing decades of accumulated gold.

60
00:04:28,804 --> 00:04:30,461
Second: Agrarian tax decentralization.

61
00:04:30,461 --> 00:04:36,260
The king did not simply spend gold; he re-engineered the economy of the region.

62
00:04:36,260 --> 00:04:44,959
By issuing royal copper-plate grants, he transferred the tax revenues of dozens of surrounding farming villages directly to the temple works.

63
00:04:44,959 --> 00:04:54,899
Every week, convoys of bullock carts arrived at Elapura carrying tons of rice, lentils, dried cow dung, and sesame oil to sustain the workforce.

64
00:04:54,899 --> 00:04:57,385
Third: The merchant guilds, the Shrenis.

65
00:04:57,385 --> 00:05:03,598
Wealthy caravan syndicates operating along the Silk Road branches poured private fortunes into the temple.

66
00:05:03,598 --> 00:05:13,124
In exchange for funding, merchants received royal tax exemptions, toll-free passage through mountain passes, and sacred merit in the eyes of the gods.

67
00:05:13,124 --> 00:05:22,237
Even the Buddhist and Hindu monasteries on the ridge functioned as financial escrow hubs, managing endowments and lending credit to caravan travelers.

68
00:05:22,237 --> 00:05:24,308
The war chest was full.

69
00:05:24,308 --> 00:05:26,379
The supply lines were locked.

70
00:05:26,379 --> 00:05:31,349
Now, Kokasa needed to assemble the greatest engineering guild in human history.

71
00:05:31,349 --> 00:05:40,180
Over the course of three months, hundreds of specialized artisans and their families arrived at the base of the Charanandri ridge.

72
00:05:40,180 --> 00:05:42,704
Kokasa did not use slave labor.

73
00:05:42,704 --> 00:05:48,171
This was a professional hereditary guild of freemen, organized into strict technical hierarchies.

74
00:05:48,171 --> 00:05:52,798
At the top were the Sutradharas, the Holders of the String.

75
00:05:52,798 --> 00:05:57,844
The elite geometricians who would translate three-dimensional temple geometry into raw stone.

76
00:05:57,844 --> 00:06:01,208
Beneath them were the Takshakas, the stone hewers.

77
00:06:01,208 --> 00:06:07,938
Men with iron wrists who understood how to shatter volcanic basalt without cracking the strata below.

78
00:06:07,938 --> 00:06:11,302
Waiting in reserve were the Rupakaras, the sculptors.

79
00:06:11,302 --> 00:06:17,611
Artists whose chisels would liberate dancing deities, charging elephants, and celestial maidens from the rock.

80
00:06:17,611 --> 00:06:20,555
But without weapons, the army was useless.

81
00:06:20,555 --> 00:06:23,499
Diamond-hard basalt destroys iron tools in hours.

82
00:06:23,499 --> 00:06:29,387
Day and night, specialized blacksmiths kept charcoal furnaces blazing at twelve hundred degrees Celsius.

83
00:06:29,387 --> 00:06:37,378
They forged high-carbon wootz-type steel chisels, quenching red-hot iron in oil and water to give the tips diamond-like hardness.

84
00:06:37,378 --> 00:06:46,631
Three tools were perfected: the pointed punch for bulk splitting, the claw chisel for leveling, and the flat gouge for fine sculpture.

85
00:06:46,631 --> 00:06:50,415
Ten thousand iron chisels were stacked ready for battle.

86
00:06:50,415 --> 00:06:52,097
The tools were forged.

87
00:06:52,097 --> 00:06:53,780
The army stood waiting.

88
00:06:53,780 --> 00:07:03,339
At dawn on the auspicious day of the full moon, Kokasa and his senior surveyors climbed to the flat summit of the Charanandri ridge.

89
00:07:03,339 --> 00:07:06,525
Their first act was not carving, but astronomy.

90
00:07:06,525 --> 00:07:13,694
In the center of the summit, they drove a tapered wooden pole into the bedrock: the sacred Shanku.

91
00:07:13,694 --> 00:07:19,270
Using a cord tied to the pole, they etched a circle in the rock.

92
00:07:19,270 --> 00:07:27,236
As the sun rose and set, the shadow touched the circle at dawn and dusk, revealing the true East-West line.

93
00:07:27,236 --> 00:07:32,812
This established the sacred axis: Kailasa would face exact West, greeting the setting sun.

94
00:07:32,812 --> 00:07:41,176
The surveyors held a long twisted cord soaked in brilliant red vermilion powder taut across eighty meters of flat rock summit.

95
00:07:41,176 --> 00:07:46,354
The surveyor pulled the cord three feet into the air, and released it.

96
00:07:46,354 --> 00:07:49,939
A razor-straight red line cut across the volcanic stone.

97
00:07:49,939 --> 00:07:50,735
Then another.

98
00:07:50,735 --> 00:07:51,532
And another.

99
00:07:51,532 --> 00:07:58,303
They laid out the master boundary: an enormous U-shaped trench footprint: eighty-four meters long, forty-seven meters wide.

100
00:07:58,303 --> 00:08:04,676
Between those trenches lay a colossal monolith of isolated mountain: fifty meters long, thirty-three meters deep.

101
00:08:04,676 --> 00:08:07,862
Inside that uncarved stone was the entire temple.

102
00:08:07,862 --> 00:08:12,641
To guarantee the trenches descended perfectly vertical, they used water leveling troughs.

103
00:08:12,641 --> 00:08:17,819
If one cut drifted by even three inches, a column below would shatter.

104
00:08:17,819 --> 00:08:21,404
By sunset, the mountain was chained in sacred geometry.

105
00:08:21,404 --> 00:08:24,988
The blueprint was physically etched into the living earth.

106
00:08:24,988 --> 00:08:32,040
Kokasa returned to the imperial court carrying a miniature terracotta model of the mountain, carved with its descending trenches.

107
00:08:32,040 --> 00:08:34,268
King Krishna gazed upon the miniature.

108
00:08:34,268 --> 00:08:38,351
For the first time, he saw how the miracle would work.

109
00:08:38,351 --> 00:08:42,434
He looked toward the inner chambers where his queen lay fading.

110
00:08:42,434 --> 00:08:43,177
Seven days.

111
00:08:43,177 --> 00:08:45,404
The promise had to be kept.

112
00:08:45,404 --> 00:08:47,631
He called for the royal seal.

113
00:08:47,631 --> 00:08:55,426
King Krishna pressed his heavy gold signet ring, the winged eagle Garuda, emblem of the Rashtrakutas, into the molten red wax.

114
00:08:55,426 --> 00:09:04,706
The royal decree was sealed: 'Let the mountain of Elapura be fashioned into the abode of the Lord of Kailasa.' Kokasa accepted the copper plate.

115
00:09:04,706 --> 00:09:06,191
He did not smile.

116
00:09:06,191 --> 00:09:12,502
He knew that from this moment on, every blow of the hammer was a fight against time.

117
00:09:12,502 --> 00:09:17,327
That night, an endless serpentine river of torches climbed the road to Charanandri.

118
00:09:17,327 --> 00:09:19,183
The quarrymen took their stations.

119
00:09:19,183 --> 00:09:22,685
Dawn, the seventh day before the queen's deadline.

120
00:09:22,685 --> 00:09:25,750
Total silence descended upon the mountain ridge.

121
00:09:25,750 --> 00:09:34,945
At the exact center of the grid, Kokasa placed the point of a heavy steel punch against the virgin black basalt.

122
00:09:34,945 --> 00:09:37,573
Beside him stood the master striker.

123
00:09:37,573 --> 00:09:41,076
A seasoned quarryman with arms like iron bands.

124
00:09:41,076 --> 00:09:45,893
He raised a thirty-pound basalt sledgehammer high into the morning air.

125
00:09:45,893 --> 00:09:48,520
Four hundred thousand tons of mountain.

126
00:09:48,520 --> 00:09:50,709
Hundreds of thousands of man-hours.

127
00:09:50,709 --> 00:09:53,774
It all began with a single breath.

128
00:09:53,774 --> 00:09:55,088
Iron met basalt.

129
00:09:55,088 --> 00:09:57,277
And the living stone cracked.

130
00:09:57,277 --> 00:10:08,661
In Episode Two: Kokasa unleashes the ancient world's most destructive engineering weapon, fire and cold water, to carve three colossal trenches thirty meters into the earth.

131
00:10:08,661 --> 00:10:11,289
Subscribe, and step into the fire.
